          "snippet": "The passages analyze the conceptual distinctions and tensions between negative liberty, defined as freedom from external constraints, and positive liberty, which involves internal capacities or collective self-mastery. Isaiah Berlin argues that these concepts are rival interpretations of political idealism, warning that positive liberty risks authoritarianism by justifying coercion in the name of true rationality. While some theorists propose alternative frameworks like republican non-domination or MacCallum's unified conception to resolve these dichotomies, debates persist regarding their dis…"
