          "score": 0.6029554,
          "snippet": "Isaiah Berlin argues for the primacy of negative liberty over positive liberty, contending that the latter's association with rational self-rule and a singular public interest threatens ethical pluralism. He posits that while national belonging addresses basic human needs, it must not be conflated with political freedom to avoid authoritarian outcomes. Consequently, society should prioritize negative liberty as essential for allowing individuals to pursue diverse genuine values without imposing a single conception of the good."
